  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Two radioactive materials <math><msub><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> have decay constants <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mi>λ</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>λ</mi></math> respectively. If initially, they have the same number of nuclei, then the ratio of the number of nuclei of <math><msub><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1&nbsp;</mn></mrow></msub></math>to that of <math><msub><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mn>1</mn><mi>e</mi></mfrac></math> after a time</p>\r",
    "image": null
  },
